    "q-41780": {
      "dom_id": "step-q41780",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-7 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=12\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}14 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 29\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ -19\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{150}[\\begin{aligned}29 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ -19\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
