      "problem": "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 21\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{42}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
