      "kp_id": "e-7429"
    },
    "q-85281": {
      "dom_id": "step-q85281",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=3\\sqrt{3}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
